% ============================================================================
% respiratory-part-alt-name — the everyday ALTERNATE name a source gives for a
% respiratory part, decoded from clauses already sitting unused inside
% `respiratory-parts.adj`'s own already-quoted NCI SEER source sentences
% (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `respiratory-parts.adj`
% (`part_function(part, function)`, trachea → main_airway,
% alveoli → gas_exchange): two of that table's own per-row quoted spans name
% NOT ONLY the part's stated function but ALSO an everyday alternate name for
% the part itself -- a fact the part/function schema had no room for:
%
%     "The trachea, commonly called the windpipe, is the main airway to the
%      lungs."
%     "The bronchi branch into smaller and smaller passageways until they
%      terminate in tiny air sacs called alveoli."
%
% New `respiratory_part_alt_name(part, alt_name)` table decodes those two
% spans as their own rows: trachea → windpipe, alveoli → air_sacs.
% "Alt_name" (an everyday synonym for the part) is categorically distinct
% from the parent table's "function" (the part's stated job) -- the trachea's
% function is main_airway, but its alternate name is a separate fact the
% function column has no room for. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? respiratory_part_alt_name(trachea, $AltName)   % windpipe
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `respiratory_part_alt_name(part, alt_name)` carrying THAT ROW'S OWN citation
% -- its own `source` and its own `locator` (RS-5e, #14986) -- with `trust`
% inherited from the envelope, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query -- the engine returns the alternate name WITH its source, on the CPU,
% with zero model calls, and abstains on anything that is not one of these two
% parts (it never invents an alternate name). The query also runs BACKWARD --
% bind an alternate name and recall the part it names:
%
%     ? respiratory_part_alt_name($Part, air_sacs)   % alveoli
%
% A recall on `larynx` -- a real, already-tabled part in `respiratory-parts.adj`,
% but whose own quoted span ("The larynx plays an essential role in human
% speech.") states only its function, never an everyday alternate name --
% correctly abstains rather than inventing one for it. Likewise `nose`,
% `pharynx`, `lungs`, and `diaphragm` are deliberately left unrowed: their own
% quoted spans in the parent table never supply a second, everyday name for
% the part itself.
%
% Provenance: EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN span as its `source` AND ITS OWN
% `locator` (RS-5e, #14986); `trust` is inherited from the envelope, one tier
% for both SEER pages. The two sentences are the same two already quoted inside
% `respiratory-parts.adj`'s own header, decoding a different clause of each --
% the alternate NAME rather than the stated function.
%
% THIS FIXED TWO DEFECTS IN ONE ROW, not one. Until this change the envelope WAS
% the trachea sentence, so an `alveoli` recall was warranted by a sentence about
% the TRACHEA -- and the single shipped `locator` was larynx.html, so that answer
% was also ADDRESSED TO A PAGE ITS SENTENCE IS NOT ON. Measured on one run of the
% 3-query companion, 2026-09-16:
%
%                                      before   after
%     trachea span                       x4       x2
%     alveoli span                       x0       x2
%     larynx.html                        x4       x2
%     bronchi.html                       x0       x2
%     alveoli answer on trachea span     x1       x0
%
% The corroborations arrays were EMPTY before this change and remain so -- 2
% citations arrays, 4 empty, 0 non-empty, both before and after. Nothing here is
% a corroboration claim. The envelope span reaches ZERO answers in both captures
% and so does the module-index locator: every row overrides both fields.
%
% TWO PAGES FOR TWO ROWS, so BOTH rows restate their `locator`. ADJ-TABLES §4
% says restate a row locator only when its page DIFFERS from the envelope's;
% here the envelope is the module index and neither row is on it. (The parent's
% `lungs` row inherits for exactly the opposite reason -- its sentence IS on the
% index. No row here is in that position.)
%
% SPANS WERE EXTRACTED FROM THE PAGE BYTES, not lifted from this header. Each
% occurs EXACTLY ONCE on its own page, ZERO times on the other two, and ZERO
% times against a REAL 404 control -- this host returns a 19,393-byte 404
% against 41-46 KB real pages, so absence here is a real absence, not the
% content-based-only kind that `oceans` and `plant-parts` are limited to.
%
% HOW they were counted belongs to the claim, because the recipe decides two of
% the three. Two recipes that both collapse whitespace and differ in one thing
% only, what an inline tag becomes: send it to the EMPTY string and all three
% spans score x1; send it to a SPACE and the trachea and alveoli spans score x0.
% The envelope scores x1 under both. So those zeros are the INSTRUMENT, not the
% pages. Shards 03600 and 03630 found the same split: SEER for 03600, nps.gov
% for 03630. That is TWO hosts across THREE tables -- and 03600 is on THIS
% file's own host, so it is not an "other host" at all. (An earlier draft of
% this line said "two other hosts", which was wrong in both halves: it
% re-read 03630's "a different host (SEER)" -- different from NPS, said from
% 03630's vantage point -- as though it were said from this file's.)
%
% The paragraph this replaces said the spans reproduced the parent
% "byte-for-byte ... no new WebFetch". That is retired: they ARE now measured
% against the pages themselves, which is a stronger statement than the one it
% was avoiding making.
%
% WHAT THIS TABLE CLAIMS, AND ITS CEILING. Nothing here carries a `quote ... at
% <byte_offset> snapshot "<sha256>"` annotation, the repo's byte-provenance
% mechanism. The spans are labelled with the page they were read from and
% nothing more: this table claims no more than `source_labeled`.
%
% `trust authoritative` -- the same tier the parent table already carries (NCI
% SEER Training is a primary U.S. government teaching resource).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table respiratory_part_alt_name {
    columns part, alt_name

    row (trachea, windpipe) {
        source "The trachea, commonly called the windpipe, is the main airway to the lungs."
        locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/respiratory/passages/larynx.html"
    }
    row (alveoli, air_sacs) {
        source "The bronchi branch into smaller and smaller passageways until they terminate in tiny air sacs called alveoli."
        locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/respiratory/passages/bronchi.html"
    }

    source "Respiration is the sequence of events that results in the exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide between the atmosphere and the body cells."
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/respiratory/"
    trust authoritative
}
